// =============================================================================
// Detection: Device-code flow sign-in (device code phishing / token theft)
// Lab:       Contoso Health Identity Platform  (lab/ - ITDR pack)
// SPL twin:  lab/splunk/detections/device-code-signin.spl (same logic/window)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CATCHES
//   Any sign-in that used the OAuth device authorization grant, or a token
//   refresh from a session that STARTED with one. Device code phishing sends
//   the victim a real Microsoft sign-in page with the attacker's code (a fake
//   "Teams meeting" invite is the usual lure). The victim signs in and passes
//   MFA, and the attacker's polling client receives the tokens. Microsoft
//   Threat Intelligence reported Storm-2372 using it at scale (blog
//   2025-02-13). A hospital has almost no legitimate use for the flow
//   (shared-display devices at most), so CA010 blocks it with the
//   authentication-flows condition (CISA SCuBA MS.AAD.3.9;
//   modules/conditional-access, enable_device_code_block). CA010 is BUILT and
//   stays report-only until it graduates (evidence-capture-plan.md, E38b).
//   This rule is that policy's detective twin:
//     before the policy is enforced -> every device-code session is seen;
//     after                          -> Successes > 0 means the block has a
//                                       gap (exclusion, report-only, an app
//                                       outside its scope).
//   Two signals, both in the sign-in logs:
//     AuthenticationProtocol == "deviceCode"      the grant itself (usually
//                                                  interactive, SigninLogs)
//     OriginalTransferMethod == "deviceCodeFlow"  every later token from that
//                                                  session (often non-
//                                                  interactive refreshes), so
//                                                  a stolen session that keeps
//                                                  refreshing stays visible
//   One row per user + source IP + signal over the look-back.
//
// SEVERITY (dynamic - the Severity column)
//   High   : tokens were issued (Successes > 0) AND the user is privileged or
//            PIM-eligible (PrivilegedUserIds, else the naming convention), or
//            the sign-in carried medium/high sign-in risk.
//   Medium : tokens were issued to anyone else.
//   Low    : no tokens issued (blocked by CA or failed), or every app is on
//            the SanctionedDeviceCodeAppIds list. Still alerted: blocked
//            attempts are how a running phishing campaign shows up.
//
// TABLE / SOURCE
//   SigninLogs + AADNonInteractiveUserSignInLogs. Microsoft Learn (table
//   reference, checked 2026-10-20): AuthenticationProtocol is a string with
//   the value deviceCode in both tables; OriginalTransferMethod is a string in
//   both. The value "deviceCodeFlow" comes from the Graph beta signIn resource
//   (originalTransferMethods: none, deviceCodeFlow, authenticationTransfer).
//   verify: the exact OriginalTransferMethod text Log Analytics stores, on the
//   first device-code test sign-in.
//   Privileged users: PrivilegedUserIds, the same Terraform-rendered id list
//   auth-method-changed-by-admin.kql uses (modules/monitoring
//   var.privileged_user_ids, from Find-ShadowAdmins.ps1 -PrivilegedIdsPath).
//
// LICENSE
//   Sign-in logs: Free per Learn's monitoring-and-health licensing table
//   (verify: Free tenants can route them through diagnostic settings - see
//   modules/entra-diagnostics main.tf LICENSING). Sign-in risk needs P2
//   (else "hidden"). Blocking the flow with CA needs P1.
//
// MITRE ATT&CK
//   T1528 Steal Application Access Token (CredentialAccess)
//   T1566.002 Phishing: Spearphishing Link (InitialAccess)
//   T1078.004 Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts (DefenseEvasion, Persistence)
//
// FALSE-POSITIVE NOTES
//   - Admins using device code for CLI tools on headless boxes (Azure CLI,
//     Graph PowerShell -UseDeviceCode, the lab's own
//     Invoke-BaselineAssessment.ps1 -UseDeviceCode). The CA block removes
//     this habit; until then it is Medium, or High for a privileged account,
//     on purpose.
//   - Shared meeting-room or display devices that genuinely need the flow:
//     put their client app ids in SanctionedDeviceCodeAppIds (Low, never
//     hidden) and exclude them in the CA policy by the same list.
//
// TUNABLE
//   LookBack 1h. SanctionedDeviceCodeAppIds empty. Privileged = object id in
//   PrivilegedUserIds (break-glass, members / eligible members of the PIM
//   groups, directory-role holders) OR the naming convention (bg-admin-NN,
//   tier0-admin-NN). An empty id list only LOWERS severity for admins named
//   otherwise; it never hides a row.
//
// SUGGESTED SENTINEL RULE
//   Frequency 5m · Lookback 1h · one alert per row · Severity column ·
//   Entities: Account = UserPrincipalName / UserId, IP = IPAddress.
//
// AUTOMATED RESPONSE
//   Notify on every incident; containment card on High (the signed-in USER:
//   revoke refresh tokens - a device-code session lives on its refresh
//   token, so revoking sessions is the actual fix). Then reset the user's
//   MFA-protected session state and check for a device registered from the
//   same IP (Storm-2372 registered devices after the phish).
// =============================================================================

let LookBack = 1h;
let SanctionedDeviceCodeAppIds = dynamic([]);
// Privileged users by OBJECT ID (rendered by modules/monitoring, as in
// auth-method-changed-by-admin.kql; committed empty, so a standalone run falls
// back to naming only).
let PrivilegedUserIds = dynamic([]); // @@PRIVILEGED_USER_IDS@@
// SCHEMA TRAP (as in break-glass-signin.kql): LocationDetails and the CA
// policy list are dynamic in SigninLogs but string in the non-interactive
// table, so each leg projects the same names and types before the union.
union
    (SigninLogs
        | where TimeGenerated > ago(LookBack)
        | where AuthenticationProtocol =~ "deviceCode" or OriginalTransferMethod =~ "deviceCodeFlow"
        | project TimeGenerated, SignInType = "interactive", UserPrincipalName, UserId, IPAddress,
                  AppDisplayName, AppId, ResourceDisplayName, AuthenticationProtocol,
                  OriginalTransferMethod, ResultType, ConditionalAccessStatus, RiskLevelDuringSignIn,
                  Country = tostring(LocationDetails.countryOrRegion),
                  CaPolicyText = tostring(ConditionalAccessPolicies), CorrelationId),
    (AADNonInteractiveUserSignInLogs
        | where TimeGenerated > ago(LookBack)
        | where AuthenticationProtocol =~ "deviceCode" or OriginalTransferMethod =~ "deviceCodeFlow"
        | project TimeGenerated, SignInType = "non-interactive", UserPrincipalName, UserId, IPAddress,
                  AppDisplayName, AppId, ResourceDisplayName, AuthenticationProtocol,
                  OriginalTransferMethod, ResultType, ConditionalAccessStatus, RiskLevelDuringSignIn,
                  Country = tostring(todynamic(tostring(LocationDetails)).countryOrRegion),
                  CaPolicyText = tostring(ConditionalAccessPolicies), CorrelationId)
| extend Via = iff(AuthenticationProtocol =~ "deviceCode", "device code grant",
                   "token from a device-code session")
| extend RiskN = case(RiskLevelDuringSignIn =~ "high", 3, RiskLevelDuringSignIn =~ "medium", 2,
                      RiskLevelDuringSignIn =~ "low", 1, 0)
// A report-only block policy logs "reportOnlyFailure" but lets the sign-in
// through: proof the planned policy would have stopped it once enforced.
| extend ReportOnlyWouldBlock = CaPolicyText has "reportOnlyFailure"
| extend UserPrincipalName = tolower(UserPrincipalName)
| summarize TimeGenerated   = max(TimeGenerated),
            FirstSeen       = min(TimeGenerated),
            SignIns         = count(),
            Successes       = countif(ResultType == "0"),
            CaBlocks        = countif(ConditionalAccessStatus == "failure"),
            AnyReportOnlyWouldBlock = max(toint(ReportOnlyWouldBlock)),
            MaxRiskN        = max(RiskN),
            SignInTypes     = make_set(SignInType, 2),
            Apps            = make_set(AppDisplayName, 10),
            AppIds          = make_set(AppId, 10),
            Resources       = make_set(ResourceDisplayName, 10),
            ErrorCodes      = make_set(ResultType, 10),
            Countries       = make_set(Country, 10),
            CorrelationIds  = make_set(CorrelationId, 10)
            by UserPrincipalName, UserId, IPAddress, Via
| extend PrivilegedById   = isnotempty(UserId) and UserId in (PrivilegedUserIds)
| extend PrivilegedByName = UserPrincipalName matches regex @"(?i)^(bg-admin-[0-9]{2}|tier0-admin-[0-9]{2})@"
| extend PrivilegedTarget = PrivilegedById or PrivilegedByName
| extend Sanctioned = array_length(SanctionedDeviceCodeAppIds) > 0
                      and array_length(set_difference(AppIds, SanctionedDeviceCodeAppIds)) == 0
| extend MaxSignInRisk = case(MaxRiskN == 3, "high", MaxRiskN == 2, "medium", MaxRiskN == 1, "low", "none")
| extend Outcome = case(
    Successes > 0, "SUCCEEDED - device-code tokens issued",
    CaBlocks > 0,  "Blocked by CA",
    "Failed - no tokens issued")
| extend Severity = case(
    Successes > 0 and (PrivilegedTarget or MaxRiskN >= 2), "High",
    Successes > 0 and Sanctioned,                          "Low",
    Successes > 0,                                         "Medium",
    "Low")
| project TimeGenerated, Severity, Outcome, Via, UserPrincipalName, UserId, IPAddress, PrivilegedTarget,
          PrivilegedById, MaxSignInRisk, SignIns, Successes, CaBlocks, AnyReportOnlyWouldBlock, Sanctioned,
          SignInTypes, Apps, AppIds, Resources, ErrorCodes, Countries, FirstSeen, CorrelationIds
| sort by TimeGenerated desc
